n to change notification settings\nL118:   * cite63†Fork 108 L119:   * cite63†Star L120:   * cite62†Code L121:   * cite64†Issues 39 L122:   * cite65†Pull requests 2 L123:   * cite66†Actions L124:   * cite67†Projects L125:   * cite68†Security and quality 0 L126:   * cite69†Insights L127: \nL128: Additional navigation options\nL129: \nL130: main\nL131: \nL132: cite70†Branches cite71†Tags L133: \nL134: [Input: Go to file]\nL135: \nL136: Go to file\nL137: \nL138: Code\nL139: \nL140: Open more actions menu\nL141: ## Latest commit\nL142: \nL143: \nL144: \nL145: ## History\nL146: \nL147: 9 Commits\nL148: ## Folders and files\nL149: Name  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\nL150: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL151: cite72†assets | cite72†assets |    |\nL152: cite73†examples | cite73†examples |    |\nL153: cite74†src | cite74†src |    |\nL154: cite75†.gitignore | cite75†.gitignore |    |\nL155: cite76†LICENSE | cite76†LICENSE |    |\nL156: cite77†MiMo-Audio-Technical-Report.pdf | cite77†MiMo-Audio-Technical-Report.pdf |    |\nL157: cite78†README.md | cite78†README.md |    |\nL158: cite79†inference_example_pretrain.py | cite79†inference_example_pretrain.py |    |\nL159: cite80†inference_example_sft.py | cite80†inference_example_sft.py |    |\nL160: cite81†requirements.txt | cite81†requirements.txt |    |\nL161: cite82†run_mimo_audio.py | cite82†run_mimo_audio.py |    |\nL162: [Button: View all files]\nL163: ## Repository files navigation\nL164: \nL165:   *   * cite83†README L166:   * cite83†Apache-2.0 license L167: \nL168: More items\nL169: \nL170: cite84†Image: Xiaomi-MiMo L171: \nL172: ### ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\nL173: MiMo Audio: Audio Language Models are Few-Shot Learners\nL174: ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\nL175: \nL176: | cite85†🤗 HuggingFace†huggingface.co | cite77†📄 Paper | cite86†📰 Blog†xiaomimimo.github.io | cite87†📊 MiMo-Audio-Eval |\nL177: ## Introduction\nL178: Existing audio language models typically rely on task-specific fine-tuning to accomplish particular audio tasks. In contrast, humans are able to generalize to new audio tasks with only a few examples or simple instructions. GPT-3 has shown that scaling next-token prediction pretraining enables strong generalization capabilities in text, and we believe this paradigm is equally applicable to the audio domain.\nL179: By scaling MiMo-Audio's pretraining data to over one hundred million of hours, we observe the emergence of few-shot learning capabilities across a diverse set of audio tasks. We develop a systematic evaluation of these capabilities and find that MiMo-Audio-7B-Base achieves SOTA performance on both speech intelligence and audio understanding benchmarks among open-source models.\nL180: Beyond standard metrics, MiMo-Audio-7B-Base generalizes to tasks absent from its training data, such as voice conversion, style transfer, and speech editing. MiMo-Audio-7B-Base also demonstrates powerful speech continuation capabilities, capable of generating highly realistic talk shows, recitations, livestreaming and debates. At the post-training stage, we curate a diverse instruction-tuning corpus and introduce thinking mechanisms into both audio understanding and generation.\nL181: MiMo-Audio-7B-Instruct achieves open-source SOTA on audio understanding benchmarks, spoken dialogue benchmarks and instruct-TTS evaluations, approaching or surpassing closed-source models.\nL182: ## Architecture\nL183: ### MiMo-Audio-Tokenizer\nL184: \nL185: MiMo-Audio-Tokenizer is a 1.2B-parameter Transformer operating at 25 Hz. It employs an eight-layer RVQ stack to generate 200 tokens per second. By jointly optimizing semantic and reconstruction objectives, we train MiMo-Audio-Tokenizer from scratch on a 10-million-hour corpus, achieving superior reconstruction quality and facilitating downstream language modeling.\nL186: MiMo-Audio couples a patch encoder, an LLM, and a patch decoder to improve modeling efficiency for high-rate sequences and bridge the length mismatch between speech and text. The patch encoder aggregates four consecutive time steps of RVQ tokens into a single patch, downsampling the sequence to a 6.25 Hz representation for the LLM. The patch decoder autoregressively generates the full 25 Hz RVQ token sequence via a delayed-generation scheme.\nL187: ### MiMo-Audio\nL188: \nL189: ## Explore MiMo-Audio Now! 🚀🚀🚀\nL190: \nL191:   * 🎧 Try the Hugging Face demo: cite88†MiMo-Audio Demo†huggingface.co L192:   * 📰 Read the Official Blog: cite86†MiMo-Audio Blog†xiaomimimo.github.io L193:   * 📄 Dive into the Technical Report: cite77†MiMo-Audio Technical Report L194: ## Model Download\nL195: Models  | 🤗 Hugging Face\nL196: --- | ---\nL197: MiMo-Audio-Tokenizer  | cite89†XiaomiMiMo/MiMo-Audio-Tokenizer†huggingface.co L198: MiMo-Audio-7B-Base  | cite90†XiaomiMiMo/MiMo-Audio-7B-Base†huggingface.co L199: MiMo-Audio-7B-Instruct  | cite91†XiaomiMiMo/MiMo-Audio-7B-Instruct†huggingface.co L200: \nL201:     pip install huggingface-hub\nL202: \nL203:     hf download XiaomiMiMo/MiMo-Audio-Tokenizer --local-dir ./models/MiMo-Audio-Tokenizer\nL204:     hf download XiaomiMiMo/MiMo-Audio-7B-Base --local-dir ./models/MiMo-Audio-7B-Base\nL205:     hf download XiaomiMiMo/MiMo-Audio-7B-Instruct --local-dir ./models/MiMo-Audio-7B-Instruct\nL206: ## Getting Started\nL207: \nL208: Spin up the MiMo-Audio demo in minutes with the built-in Gradio app.\nL209: \nL210: ### Prerequisites (Linux)\nL211: \nL212:   * Python 3.12\nL213:   * CUDA >= 12.0\nL214: ### Installation\nL215: \nL216:     git clone https://github.com/XiaomiMiMo/MiMo-Audio.git\nL217:     cd MiMo-Audio\nL218:     pip install -r requirements.txt\nL219:     pip install flash-attn==2.7.4.post1\nL220: \nL221: Note\nL222: \nL223: If the compilation of flash-attn takes too long, you can download the precompiled wheel and install it manually:\nL224: \nL225:   * cite92†Download Precompiled Wheel L226: \nL227:     pip install /path/to/flash_attn-2.7.4.post1+cu12torch2.6cxx11abiFALSE-cp312-cp312-linux_x86_64.whl\nL228: ### Run the demo\nL229: \nL230:     python run_mimo_audio.py\nL231: \nL232: This launches a local Gradio interface where you can try MiMo-Audio interactively.\nL233: \nL234: Enter the local paths for `MiMo-Audio-Tokenizer` and `MiMo-Audio-7B-Instruct`, then enjoy the full functionality of MiMo-Audio!\nL235: \nL236: ## Inference Scripts\nL237: \nL238: ### Base Model\nL239: \nL240: We provide an example script to explore the in-context learning capabilities of `MiMo-Audio-7B-Base`.\nL241: See: cite79†`inference_example_pretrain.py` L242: ### Instruct Model\nL243: \nL244: To try the instruction-tuned model `MiMo-Audio-7B-Instruct`, use the corresponding inference script.\nL245: See: cite80†`inference_example_sft.py` L246: \nL247: ## Evaluation Toolkit\nL248: \nL249: Full evaluation suite are available at 🌐cite87†MiMo-Audio-Eval .\nL250: \nL251: This toolkit is designed to evaluate MiMo-Audio and other recent audio LLMs as mentioned in the paper. It provides a flexible and extensible framework, supporting a wide range of datasets, tasks, and models.\nL252: ## Citation\nL253: \nL254:     @misc{coreteam2025mimoaudio,\nL255:           title={MiMo-Audio: Audio Language Models are Few-Shot Learners},\nL256:           author={LLM-Core-Team Xiaomi},\nL257:           year={2025},\nL258:           url={https://github.com/XiaomiMiMo/MiMo-Audio},\nL259:     }\nL260: \nL261: ## Contact\nL262: \nL263: Please contact us at mimo@xiaomi.com or open an issue if you have any questions.\nL264: \nL265: ## About\nL266: \nL267: MiMo-Audio: Audio Language Models are Few-Shot Learners\nL268: \nL269: cite93†xiaomimimo.github.io/MiMo-Audio-Demo/†xiaomimimo.github.io L270: ### Resources\nL271: \nL272: cite94†Readme L273: \nL274: cite95†Apache-2.0 license L275: \nL276: cite96†Activity L277: \nL278: cite97†Custom properties L279: \nL280: ### Stars\nL281: \nL282: 1.1k stars\nL283: \nL284: ### Watchers\nL285: \nL286: 17 watching\nL287: \nL288: ### Forks\nL289: \nL290: cite98†108 forks L291: \nL292: cite99†Report repository L293: \nL294: ## Releases\nL295: \nL296: ## Packages\nL297: \nL298: ## Contributors\nL299: \nL300: ## Languages\nL301: \nL302: You can’t perform that action at this time.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn3383view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://xiaomimimo.github.io/MiMo-Audio\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: URL https://xiaomimimo.github.io/MiMo-Audio is not accessible via this tool.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDeep researcher with test-time diffusion (https://research.google/blog/deep-researcher-with-test-time-diffusion/)\nciteturn3383view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn3382view0\",\"id\":108}); Total lines: 194\nL0: cite0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com L1: ## Explore our many areas of focus\nL2: \nL3: cite1†Explore all research areas L4: \nL5: Applied AI & sciences\nL6: \nL7: cite2†Earth AI cite3†Health AI cite4†Science AI cite5†Sustainability & crisis resilience L8: \nL9: Foundational ML & algorithms\nL10: \nL11: cite6†Algorithms & theory cite7†Information retrieval cite8†Machine intelligence cite9†Machine perception cite10†Natural language processing L12: \nL13: People, systems & quantum AI\nL14: cite11†Human-computer interaction and visualization cite12†Networking cite13†Quantum AI cite14†Responsible AI cite15†Anti abuse cite16†Software engineering cite17†Software systems L15: \nL16: Learn More\nL17: \nL18: cite18†Publications cite19†Projects L19: ## Building a collaborative ecosystem\nL20: \nL21: cite20†Datasets Access high-quality datasets to accelerate your research. cite21†Tools & services Explore our latest AI models and products. L22: \nL23: cite22†Open source Discover open-source code and collaborate with the community. L24: ## Shaping the future together\nL25: \nL26: cite23†See all programs L27: \nL28: cite24†Faculty programs Participating in the academic research community through meaningful engagement with university faculty. cite25†Student programs Supporting the next generation of researchers through a wide range of programming. L29: \nL30: cite26†R&D programs Collaborating with industry and academic experts to support foundational science and innovation. cite27†Locations Find your place in our global offices and research labs. L31: ## Translating discovery into real-world impact\nL32: \nL33: cite28†People Our researchers drive advancements in computer science through both fundamental and applied research. L34: \nL35: cite29†Teams Collaborative groups tackling the world's most challenging AI problems. L36: \nL37: Research\nL38: ## Explore our many areas of focus\nL39: \nL40: cite1†Explore all research areas L41: \nL42: Applied AI & sciences\nL43: \nL44: cite2†Earth AI cite3†Health AI cite4†Science AI cite5†Sustainability & crisis resilience L45: \nL46: Foundational ML & algorithms\nL47: \nL48: cite6†Algorithms & theory cite7†Information retrieval cite8†Machine intelligence cite9†Machine perception cite10†Natural language processing L49: \nL50: People, systems & quantum AI\nL51: cite11†Human-computer interaction and visualization cite12†Networking cite13†Quantum AI cite14†Responsible AI cite15†Anti abuse cite16†Software engineering cite17†Software systems L52: \nL53: Learn More\nL54: \nL55: cite18†Publications cite19†Projects L56: \nL57: Resources\nL58: ## Building a collaborative ecosystem\nL59: \nL60: cite20†Datasets Access high-quality datasets to accelerate your research. cite21†Tools & services Explore our latest AI models and products. cite22†Open source Discover open-source code and collaborate with the community. L61: \nL62: cite30†Conferences & events L63: \nL64: Careers\nL65: ## Shaping the future together\nL66: \nL67: cite23†See all programs L68: \nL69: cite24†Faculty programs Participating in the academic research community through meaningful engagement with university faculty. cite25†Student programs Supporting the next generation of researchers through a wide range of programming. cite26†R&D programs Collaborating with industry and academic experts to support foundational science and innovation. cite27†Locations Find your place in our global offices and research labs. L70: \nL71: cite31†Blog L72: \nL73: About\nL74: ## Translating discovery into real-world impact\nL75: \nL76: cite28†People Our researchers drive advancements in computer science through both fundamental and applied research. cite29†Teams Collaborative groups tackling the world's most challenging AI problems. L77: \nL78: cite32†Google Research L79: \nL80: cite33†Google AI Learn about all our AI†ai.google cite34†Google DeepMind Explore the frontier of AI†deepmind.google cite35†Google Labs Try our AI experiments†labs.google L81: \nL82: [Button: Research ]\nL83: \nL84: [Button: Resources ]\nL85: \nL86: cite30†Conferences & events L87: [Button: Careers ]\nL88: \nL89: cite31†Blog L90: \nL91: [Button: About ]\nL92: \nL93: [Input: Search]\nL94: \nL95: Search\nL96: \nL97: cite36†Image: Deep researcher with test-time diffusion†storage.googleapis.com L98: # Deep researcher with test-time diffusion\nL99: \nL100: September 19, 2025\nL101: \nL102: Rujun Han and Chen-Yu Lee, Research Scientists, Google Cloud\nL103: \nL104: We introduce Test-Time Diffusion Deep Researcher (TTD-DR), a framework that uses a Deep Research agent to draft and revise its own drafts using high-quality retrieved information. This approach achieves new state-of-the-art results in writing long-form research reports and completing complex reasoning tasks.\nL105: ## Quick links\nL106: \nL107:   * cite37†Paper†arxiv.org L108: \nL109: The recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have fueled the emergence of cite38†deep research†openai.com (DR) agents. These agents demonstrate remarkable capabilities, including the generation of cite39†novel ideas†arxiv.org , efficient cite40†information retrieval†arxiv.org , experimental execution, and the subsequent drafting of comprehensive cite41†reports†arxiv.org and cite42†academic papers†arxiv.org .\nL110: Currently, most cite43†public DR agents†github.com use a variety of clever techniques to improve their results, like cite44†performing reasoning via chain-of-thought or cite45†generating multiple answers†openreview.net and selecting the best one. While they've made impressive progress, they often bolt different tools together without considering the iterative nature of human research.\nL111: They're missing the key process (i.e., planning, drafting, researching, and iterating based on feedback) on which people rely when writing a paper about a complex topic. A key part of that revision process is to do more research to cite46†find missing information or strengthen your arguments†www.emerald.com .\nL112: This human pattern is surprisingly similar to the mechanism of cite47†retrieval†proceedings.mlr.press -augmented diffusion models that start with a “noisy” or messy output and gradually refine it into a high-quality result. What if an AI agent's rough draft is the noisy version, and a search tool acts as the denoising step that cleans it up with new facts?\nL113: Today we introduce cite37†Test-Time Diffusion Deep Researcher†arxiv.org (TTD-DR), a DR agent that imitates the way humans do research. To our knowledge, TTD-DR is the first research agent that models research report writing as a diffusion process, where a messy first draft is gradually polished into a high-quality final version. We introduce two new algorithms that work together to enable TTD-DR.\nL114: First, component-wise optimization via cite48†self-evolution†arxiv.org enhances the quality of each step in the research workflow. Then, report-level refinement via cite49†denoising with retrieval†arxiv.org applies newly retrieved information to revise and improve the report draft. We demonstrate that TTD-DR achieves state-of-the-art results on long-form report writing and multi-hop reasoning tasks.\n"
